WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.675
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Newspaper Class, level one.

2
00:00:05.975 --> 00:00:09.559
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.859 --> 00:00:16.877
Unit fifteen: Breaking News Coverage. Today you will give live updates with the present continuous and caution phrases.

4
00:00:17.177 --> 00:00:19.759
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:23.724
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.583 --> 00:00:25.770
Live update.

7
00:00:26.783 --> 00:00:30.095
Live update means new information given while an event is happening.

8
00:00:30.683 --> 00:00:33.205
A live update appeared every ten minutes.

9
00:00:33.817 --> 00:00:36.655
Your turn. Say: Live update.

10
00:00:39.500 --> 00:00:40.664
Live update.

11
00:00:41.417 --> 00:00:42.720
Developing story.

12
00:00:43.717 --> 00:00:47.249
Developing story means a news event whose facts are still changing.

13
00:00:47.850 --> 00:00:49.664
This is a developing story.

14
00:00:50.250 --> 00:00:53.042
Your turn. Say: Developing story.

15
00:00:55.900 --> 00:00:57.249
Developing story.

16
00:00:57.983 --> 00:00:59.600
Unverified report.

17
00:01:00.583 --> 00:01:04.499
Unverified report means information that has not yet been confirmed.

18
00:01:05.083 --> 00:01:08.000
An unverified report spread quickly online.

19
00:01:08.617 --> 00:01:12.024
Your turn. Say: Unverified report.

20
00:01:14.867 --> 00:01:16.541
Unverified report.

21
00:01:17.283 --> 00:01:18.610
Wire service.

22
00:01:19.617 --> 00:01:23.903
Wire service means an agency that supplies news to many newspapers.

23
00:01:24.517 --> 00:01:26.725
The wire service sent the first photos.

24
00:01:27.317 --> 00:01:30.584
Your turn. Say: Wire service.

25
00:01:33.433 --> 00:01:34.678
Wire service.

26
00:01:35.417 --> 00:01:36.336
Correction.

27
00:01:37.350 --> 00:01:40.302
Correction means a published change that fixes a mistake.

28
00:01:40.917 --> 00:01:43.543
The paper printed a correction on page two.

29
00:01:44.150 --> 00:01:46.431
Your turn. Say: Correction.

30
00:01:49.267 --> 00:01:50.210
Correction.

31
00:01:50.950 --> 00:01:52.125
News alert.

32
00:01:53.117 --> 00:01:56.393
News alert means a short message announcing important news.

33
00:01:56.983 --> 00:01:59.029
A news alert appeared on my phone.

34
00:01:59.617 --> 00:02:02.443
Your turn. Say: News alert.

35
00:02:05.300 --> 00:02:07.091
News alert.

36
00:02:07.850 --> 00:02:09.095
Rolling coverage.

37
00:02:10.083 --> 00:02:14.161
Rolling coverage means continuous reporting that is updated again and again.

38
00:02:14.750 --> 00:02:17.202
Rolling coverage kept viewers informed.

39
00:02:17.817 --> 00:02:20.678
Your turn. Say: Rolling coverage.

40
00:02:23.533 --> 00:02:25.115
Rolling coverage.

41
00:02:25.850 --> 00:02:27.339
Confirmed detail.

42
00:02:28.350 --> 00:02:31.987
Confirmed detail means a fact that has been checked and proved true.

43
00:02:32.583 --> 00:02:35.558
Each confirmed detail is added to the report.

44
00:02:36.150 --> 00:02:39.244
Your turn. Say: Confirmed detail.

45
00:02:42.100 --> 00:02:43.786
Confirmed detail.

46
00:02:44.600 --> 00:02:48.146
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:48.983 --> 00:02:56.990
Visal, a news alert says 'Police are investigating an explosion. Reports suggest two people are injured.' Why the continuous?

48
00:02:58.083 --> 00:03:05.705
The present continuous shows that events are in progress. Rolling coverage uses it for a developing story.

49
00:03:06.817 --> 00:03:09.675
Why 'reports suggest' and not 'two people are injured'?

50
00:03:10.783 --> 00:03:19.856
Because the report is unverified. Careful reporters wait for a confirmed detail from an official source or a wire service.

51
00:03:20.950 --> 00:03:23.193
What happens if the first report is wrong?

52
00:03:24.283 --> 00:03:31.461
The paper publishes a correction and says clearly what changed, so readers can trust the next update.

53
00:03:32.600 --> 00:03:34.100
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:34.950 --> 00:03:38.610
Grammar focus: Present continuous and caution phrases.

55
00:03:38.910 --> 00:03:40.073
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:40.783 --> 00:03:44.118
Police are investigating an explosion in the city centre.

57
00:03:45.017 --> 00:03:47.515
Reports suggest that two people are injured.

58
00:03:48.417 --> 00:03:51.450
The wire service is reporting that the road is closed.

59
00:03:52.400 --> 00:04:00.230
Use the present continuous for events in progress. Add 'reports suggest', 'it is understood' or 'unconfirmed' until a detail is checked.

60
00:04:00.983 --> 00:04:04.924
Be careful! Don't say: Police investigate now an event.

61
00:04:06.867 --> 00:04:10.178
Say: Police are investigating an event.

62
00:04:10.478 --> 00:04:13.523
Use the present continuous for events in progress.

63
00:04:14.333 --> 00:04:16.684
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:17.500 --> 00:04:18.757
As It Happens.

65
00:04:19.057 --> 00:04:23.541
When an explosion shakes a city, rolling coverage begins within minutes.

66
00:04:24.533 --> 00:04:29.354
A news alert says police are investigating, but the story is still developing.

67
00:04:30.367 --> 00:04:36.465
An early unverified report suggests that two people are injured, yet no confirmed detail has arrived.

68
00:04:37.467 --> 00:04:42.345
The wire service is checking each live update, and a correction follows if a fact was wrong.

69
00:04:43.333 --> 00:04:46.018
Speed matters, but accuracy matters more.

70
00:04:47.133 --> 00:04:51.425
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:52.283 --> 00:04:54.979
Police blank an explosion in the city centre.

72
00:04:55.279 --> 00:05:00.071
A, are investigating. B, investigate to. C, investigated now.

73
00:05:03.633 --> 00:05:06.550
The answer is A: are investigating.

74
00:05:07.717 --> 00:05:10.239
A fact that has been checked is a blank detail.

75
00:05:10.539 --> 00:05:14.030
A, confirmed. B, developing. C, rolling.

76
00:05:17.567 --> 00:05:19.415
The answer is A: confirmed.

77
00:05:20.550 --> 00:05:23.246
A published change that fixes a mistake is a blank.

78
00:05:23.546 --> 00:05:27.780
A, correction. B, wire service. C, news alert.

79
00:05:31.333 --> 00:05:33.240
The answer is A: correction.

80
00:05:34.383 --> 00:05:36.325
Which phrase is the most careful?

81
00:05:36.625 --> 00:05:43.285
A, Two people are dead.. B, Reports suggest two people are injured.. C, Two people died for sure..

82
00:05:46.833 --> 00:05:50.702
The answer is B: Reports suggest two people are injured..

83
00:05:51.850 --> 00:05:54.360
According to the reading, accuracy matters…

84
00:05:54.660 --> 00:05:58.848
A, less than speed. B, more than speed. C, never.

85
00:06:02.400 --> 00:06:04.748
The answer is B: more than speed.

86
00:06:05.933 --> 00:06:09.422
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:10.283 --> 00:06:13.757
Why might early breaking news reports sometimes turn out to be wrong?

88
00:06:22.550 --> 00:06:24.027
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:24.327 --> 00:06:49.740
Early reports may be wrong because reporters are working quickly and facts are still changing. Eyewitnesses can be confused or frightened, and rumours spread fast on social media. If a newspaper publishes an unverified report, it risks making a mistake. For this reason, careful journalists wait for confirmed details and publish corrections.

90
00:06:50.040 --> 00:06:51.691
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:06:52.583 --> 00:06:57.894
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and present continuous and caution phrases.

92
00:06:58.194 --> 00:07:04.051
Today's value is critical awareness. Use technology wisely, and always think for yourself.

93
00:07:04.351 --> 00:07:07.201
See you in Unit sixteen. Goodbye!

94
00:07:07.501 --> 00:07:10.153
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

